WEBVTT

NOTE Module 07 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (129.2s). en.

1
00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:02.979
Welcome to Module Seven — generative adversarial networks.

2
00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:06.831
The whole idea is a game between two networks.

3
00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:13.639
We'll cover that game, the DCGAN recipe, mode collapse, the Wasserstein fix, and how to evaluate a generator.

4
00:00:13.993 --> 00:00:14.701
Here's the setup.

5
00:00:15.055 --> 00:00:19.231
A generator maps random noise to a fake image.

6
00:00:19.588 --> 00:00:22.624
A discriminator tries to tell real from fake.

7
00:00:23.007 --> 00:00:29.406
You train the discriminator to catch the generator, and the generator to fool the discriminator.

8
00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:39.058
At the theoretical equilibrium the discriminator is reduced to guessing — fifty-fifty everywhere — but in practice that balance is famously fragile.

9
00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:55.797
DCGAN was the first reliably stable recipe: transposed convolutions in the generator, strided convolutions in the discriminator, batch norm in both, LeakyReLU, no fully-connected layers, a tanh output — and Adam with beta-one turned down to zero point five.

10
00:00:56.175 --> 00:01:03.644
The classic failure is mode collapse, where the generator gives up and produces only a few samples.

11
00:01:04.009 --> 00:01:11.670
Watch for low diversity, and fight it with mini-batch discrimination, feature matching, or an unrolled discriminator that the generator can't game.

12
00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:14.137
The bigger leap was Wasserstein GAN.

13
00:01:14.489 --> 00:01:26.349
Swap the Jensen–Shannon divergence for Earth-Mover distance, rename the discriminator a critic with a linear output, and enforce a one-Lipschitz constraint — these days with a gradient penalty.

14
00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:31.603
The payoff is huge: the loss finally correlates with sample quality.

15
00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:33.583
You'll also want control.

16
00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:46.691
Concatenate a class embedding to the noise for a conditional GAN; AC-GAN adds an auxiliary classifier; and pix2pix and CycleGAN take it all the way to image-to-image translation.

17
00:01:47.046 --> 00:01:47.401
Finally, measurement.

18
00:01:47.756 --> 00:01:52.995
The Inception Score rewards confident, diverse samples but is gameable.

19
00:01:53.342 --> 00:02:00.597
FID — the Fréchet distance between real and generated feature statistics — is the standard, where lower is better.

20
00:02:00.962 --> 00:02:03.889
Always report both, with your sample size.

21
00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:08.661
Let's train a DCGAN on MNIST — open the labs.
